Deliberately static, not DHCP: VLANs 10/20/30 (per-floor L2 mgmt), 100 (servers), 500 (AP mgmt — all three floors, plus WLC mgmt at Core). Requirement 12 explicitly calls for static management IPs; extending that same logic to the AP-mgmt VLAN keeps infrastructure addressing predictable rather than lease-dependent.

Design decision — centralization vs. per-switch DHCP: the reference requirement implies each floor's AGG switch runs its own local DHCP function. This build instead centralizes all leases at one server and relays via helper-address from the correct gateway device. Rationale: single source of truth for lease tracking, consistent DNS assignment, and one point of administration — standard practice in a real deployment of this size, and the client-facing behavior (gateway placement, subnet per floor) still matches the requirement exactly.

4. Key Architecture Decisions
L3 boundary at Aggregation, for wired department VLANs: avoids a routed boundary sitting behind an STP-blocked "cold standby" link, keeps failover fast.
HSRP: matches the redundancy model used consistently across Core and Aggregation; version 2 required network-wide once VLAN 500's group number (500) exceeded the 255 ceiling for HSRPv1.
Two-GE-port hardware constraint: every switch (access/agg/core/server) has only 2 Gigabit ports, rest FastEthernet — this shaped every uplink assignment (Gig reserved for Core-to-Core and Core-to-Edge; all Agg-to-Core and Agg-to-Access links on FastEthernet).
